 {'from': 'gpt', 'value': 'Sure! An abstract class is a class that cannot be instantiated on its own but can be subclassed. It may have abstract methods, which are declared without implementation, and concrete methods, which have an implementation. Subclasses of an abstract class must implement all its abstract methods or be declared as abstract themselves.

On the other hand, an interface is a collection of method signatures without any implementation. It defines a contract that classes that implement the interface must follow. An interface can be thought of as a blueprint for a class that specifies what methods the class must have, but not how they should be implemented.

In summary, an abstract class provides some implementation and structure, while an interface only provides a set of method signatures to be implemented by any class that implements it.'}]

